{
 "_note": "<CollapsingHeader> (U10) — the collapse maths, which is a pure function of `--scroll-y` and therefore lives beside it. FRACTION is the offset over the collapse RANGE (height - minHeight), clamped 0..1. The degenerate case answers 0, not 1, matching metrics.json: a header with no range to collapse must publish the RESTING state, because booting into the collapsed look is the visible bug. OVER-SCROLL is where the two idioms live: with `stretch` the header and image GROW by the over-scroll (the iOS idiom), without it the header pins; either way the fraction stays 0. PARALLAX is how much slower the image moves than the content, so the image's translation is scrollY * parallax — 0 moves with the content, 1 pins the image. Parallax clamps to 0..1 rather than inverting. REDUCED MOTION zeroes parallax and keeps everything else: the collapse is LAYOUT and must still happen, and `stretch` survives too because it tracks the finger one-to-one rather than animating on its own, which is not what the vestibular guidance is about. THE TITLE IS ONE ACCESSIBILITY ELEMENT AT EVERY FRACTION, and that is enforced by construction rather than by review: `titleOwner` is a single value, the header's opacity reaches 0 exactly at the hand-off point (0.75) and the nav bar's leaves 0 exactly there, so the two never both render. A naive implementation cross-fades them over the whole range and a screen reader then finds the title twice. THE PINNED SLOT RAISES THE FLOOR: effectiveMinHeight is max(minHeight, pinnedHeight), so content that must survive the collapse cannot be clipped by a minHeight the author picked before adding it. on:collapse is quantised to a hundredth of the range with the endpoints always emitted, so a handler sees exactly 0 and exactly 1.",
